# The quadratic inequalities, each a universal law over the integers, proved by the sum-of-squares certificate: the
# difference of the two sides is a sum of squares of linear forms (its Gram matrix is positive semidefinite), so it is
# non-negative for every value. These are the positivity facts the order rests on, the same inequalities Coq, Agda, and
# Lean prove from completing the square, here machine-checked by `seed test`. The proof is the certificate itself; a
# false version (an indefinite or negative-definite difference) fails to certify and is rejected.

# Every square is non-negative: a^2 >= 0, for every a. The floor of the order, the reason the integers carry a
# square-respecting order while no higher rung in the tower can.
task square-is-non-negative
  take a, like integer
  like integer
  hold <every square is at least zero>
    call is-minimum
      call multiply
        read a
        read a
      code 0
  send back
    read a

# The arithmetic-mean / geometric-mean inequality for two terms: a^2 + b^2 >= 2 a b, for every a and b. The difference
# is (a - b)^2.
task arithmetic-geometric-mean
  take a, like integer
  take b, like integer
  like integer
  hold <the sum of two squares is at least twice their product>
    call is-minimum
      call add
        call multiply
          read a
          read a
        call multiply
          read b
          read b
      call multiply
        code 2
        call multiply
          read a
          read b
  send back
    read a

# The sum of three squares dominates the cyclic sum of products: a^2 + b^2 + c^2 >= a b + b c + c a, for every a, b, c.
# The difference is one half the sum of the three squared differences.
task three-square-cyclic
  take a, like integer
  take b, like integer
  take c, like integer
  like integer
  hold <the sum of three squares is at least their cyclic product sum>
    call is-minimum
      call add
        call add
          call multiply
            read a
            read a
          call multiply
            read b
            read b
        call multiply
          read c
          read c
      call add
        call add
          call multiply
            read a
            read b
          call multiply
            read b
            read c
        call multiply
          read c
          read a
  send back
    read a

# The power-mean step for two terms: 2 a^2 + 2 b^2 >= (a + b)^2, for every a and b. The difference is (a - b)^2. This is
# why the mean of squares is at least the square of the mean.
task power-mean-two
  take a, like integer
  take b, like integer
  like integer
  hold <twice the sum of two squares is at least the square of their sum>
    call is-minimum
      call add
        call multiply
          code 2
          call multiply
            read a
            read a
        call multiply
          code 2
          call multiply
            read b
            read b
      call multiply
        call add
          read a
          read b
        call add
          read a
          read b
  send back
    read a

# A four-term sum of squares dominates two cross products: a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2 >= 2 a b + 2 c d, for every a, b, c, d.
# The difference is (a - b)^2 + (c - d)^2.
task four-square-paired
  take a, like integer
  take b, like integer
  take c, like integer
  take d, like integer
  like integer
  hold <four squares dominate twice two paired products>
    call is-minimum
      call add
        call add
          call add
            call multiply
              read a
              read a
            call multiply
              read b
              read b
          call multiply
            read c
            read c
        call multiply
          read d
          read d
      call add
        call multiply
          code 2
          call multiply
            read a
            read b
        call multiply
          code 2
          call multiply
            read c
            read d
  send back
    read a

# A four-term sum of squares dominates the cyclic sum of adjacent products: a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2 >= a b + b c + c d + d a,
# for every a, b, c, d. The difference is one half the sum of the four squared adjacent differences.
task four-square-cyclic
  take a, like integer
  take b, like integer
  take c, like integer
  take d, like integer
  like integer
  hold <four squares dominate the cyclic adjacent product sum>
    call is-minimum
      call add
        call add
          call add
            call multiply
              read a
              read a
            call multiply
              read b
              read b
          call multiply
            read c
            read c
        call multiply
          read d
          read d
      call add
        call add
          call add
            call multiply
              read a
              read b
            call multiply
              read b
              read c
          call multiply
            read c
            read d
        call multiply
          read d
          read a
  send back
    read a

# The three-term Cauchy bound: (a + b + c)^2 <= 3 (a^2 + b^2 + c^2), for every a, b, c. The difference is the sum of the
# three squared differences (a - b)^2 + (b - c)^2 + (c - a)^2, so the square of a sum never exceeds the count times the
# sum of squares. This is the power-mean inequality for three terms.
task three-term-power-mean
  take a, like integer
  take b, like integer
  take c, like integer
  like integer
  hold <the square of a three-term sum is at most three times the sum of squares>
    call is-minimum
      call multiply
        code 3
        call add
          call add
            call multiply
              read a
              read a
            call multiply
              read b
              read b
          call multiply
            read c
            read c
      call multiply
        call add
          call add
            read a
            read b
          read c
        call add
          call add
            read a
            read b
          read c
  send back
    read a
